e  interaction mechanisms between Z pages  and the open census clients or you can  do things like change the sampling  strategy inside the Z page about having  to redeploy your app we use these of  Google extensively we're actually really  excited to see how people outside of  Google use them  the next thing we have is the w3c trace  context format so about two years ago we  kicked off a small project between  Google and some Zipkin developers to  define a common trace context  propagation format that scents grown to  involve a number of other big companies  like Microsoft diner Tracy and others  context propagation as I mentioned  before is what allows you to actually  get traces across services it's that you  typically a common ID that's used the  link you know when service a talks to  service B B to C C to D and so on that  context that context will be the same  and that's how later on you can process  those to build a single trace the  challenge today is that vendors  typically use their own context  propagation formats so for stackdriver  historically we had a HTTP header called  I think X Google Cloud trace for a  Zipkin they have various HTTP pet  headers that are known as the b3 headers  and so on and so there are a lot of  benefits to having vendors align on a  common context propagation format one of  those is that if you have multiple  vendors participating in a trace let's  say you have different companies and  they want to trace a single interaction  or different parts of a company use  different backends you want those to be  able to communicate the second is sort  of black box tracing so if you have  something like a network load balancer  inside of your application that strips  headers for mange the P requests you'll  break a trace with a common context  propagation format or even just a common  header that won't happen because now we  can work with those vendors and  certainly big names like like Google  Microsoft Dino trace and others we can  achieve this where we can tell those  vendors of like load balancers and other  things to stop stripping headers or so  they stopped stripping this particular  header so the trace will will continue  as we discussed before another really  cool feature of open census is multi  exporter support so you can choose  multiple exporters at once this is  useful in situations where you have  different parts of your organization  using different backends let's say maybe  you use a combination of Zipkin and  Prometheus where you have metrics up to  one status to the other we're sorry  traces to the other and so we can handle  that or if you have even like let's say  you're using like a Zipkin and part of  the company and SolarWinds in another  part or signal FX in another part with  open census you can you can support all  those backends  you don't have to build the  instrumentation three times which is  awesome secondly you can send all the  data it's all backends so if one team  wants to use one back in another team  let's use another you could support that  with open census with very little work  and so the multi exported support is  great it lets you achieve scenarios that  we're really never possible before if I  do extreme amount of work and it also  removes a lot of stickiness where if you  do want to change headers you can do it  with just changing one line of code  which is great are there any differences  in what you can send to the different  exporters generally yeah the the nice  thing in this in this industry lease is  that generally most vendors have similar  notions of what a metric is or what it  traces and so generally everything if  you choose one vendor versus another  basically everything is gonna show up  either way cool and so in theory I guess  you could have some vendors supporting  features that others don't but most of  them all support the same feature set  and for the open census open source  project we've been very adamant that all  features need to support Zipkin and  Prometheus right so we're not going to  add any vendor specific special sauce so  there's not gonna be any sort of like  special stack driver only features and  open census that'd be really bad to the  project everything has to support the  are sort of our sanctioned open source  back-end so now I'm gonna talk about  some of the new features we're  developing for open since it's going  forward everything that I've described  thus far is for the project as it exists  today and so we've already come a long  way and had a lot of success but we're  doing a lot of work over the upcoming  quarters and years the first is really  simple we're just expanding our list of  integrations so getting support for more  client libraries more storage clients  and so on fairly straightforward the  next is this thing called the open  sentence collector service so today open  census just like the backends for  various other vendors there's like just  like the instrumentation various other  vendors since data directly to a  back-end so in that example we had of  service a service be sort of see they  send data directly to stack drive in the  demo that we gave the collector service  will be an optional component that you  can put in between that collects all the  traces and metrics from your application  and then reroute them to your backends  of choice it makes export a  configuration simpler you only have to  do it on the service instead of on each  client it also enables things like  smarter sampling so instead of randomly  say  playing 1% of traces you could sample  after the fact and capture traces that  have high latency for example or traces  associative errors and some other  interesting characteristics which is an  incredibly powerful feature like that  makes tracing dramatically more  effective the other thing it will enable  longer term is quite app support so  support for clients for like iOS Android  Windows run and web and so on where  you'll have those these clients send  telemetry to the open census service  hosted within your application which  then forwards the data back to your  back-end of choice and so that that  allows you to get like traces to have  full and end view all the way from a  front-end web app to read your bet  applications storage layer and so you  can see the actual client perceived  latency rather than just your back-end  ladies really really great next we're  going to expand to more telemetry types  so we've been talking about this for a  while  but we think we want to capture things  like logs and exceptions maybe one day  things like profiles but basically more  and more types of telemetry from from  client applications open census is not  going to be limited to just metrics and  traces that is certainly not a goal the  project we want to capture as much as we  can and send it wherever you want and  finally we want to keep growing the tent  to expand new partners so we announced  at O'Reilly velocity month or two ago  that Microsoft has joined open census  and they're doing the lion's share  development for.net they're also making  some pretty sizable contributions for  Python and I think nodejs and so we're  really excited about that signal FX is  also made and will be making some  contributions and pivotal in particular  is going to be working a lot of  Microsoft on the.net distribution and so  really excited to have them making a big  heavyweight contributions to the project  there were of course also exporters  supported and that have been developed  by third-party companies so data dog  heads they're an exporter solar wind  teaser and exporter in Stata has their  own exporter finally the community has  created exporters for a number of other  backend so for example we can send  traces to AWS x-ray some of the  community contributed that similarly for  Yeager and other backends and so we're  really excited to watch the ecosystem  start to grow open census was started by  Google as historically being a entirely  Google led project we're really excited  to see where it's gonna go if these new  partners involved and with that we're  done the main part of the presentation  so I think we're gonna say  questions we're gonna go offline for a  minute or two and then we'll start  answering questions yep thanks Morgan  and everybody on the livestream stay  tuned for the live Q&amp;A we'll be back in  less than a minute  you  welcome back everyone  we've received a number of questions  from the audience that we're gonna go  ahead and answer for you so the first  one that we got is is there any latency  introduced into the application when  leveraging open census for tracing yeah  that's a good question  we get that question a lot for open  census or any of the telemetry capture  the answer is there's effectively no  measurable latency introduced for  tracing or for metrics it's we've done  measurement since it's basically zero  keep in mind like this is tracing  metrics and census in fact are things  that we use on all services running at  Google so but just for for info you can  you can stay Google fast even while  having you know thousands of levels of  microservices instrumented with census  not a problem  so really no penalty to doing that type  of instrumentation no in general most of  these like unless you're doing things  like profiling where it's constantly on  like in general most telemetry  collection has effectively no impact on  your app if you consume a little bit of  memory maybe like five Meg's of RAM but  no nothing in the critical path for  latency awesome all right next question  what makes open census different from  say Griffin o or other monitoring tools  yes another question that we get very  often so it's really important to keep  in mind open census is just the  telemetry collection so it's just client  libraries that you link to your  application to send Lema tree to another  back-end one of those backends might be  grifone a-- one of t