er are can be pretty  confident that it's not doing some weird  thing you don't understand and it's not  poorly impacting the performance of  graph you can also go and change those  interfaces of you or some reason you  need to it's also open source which  means you can go and modify open census  if there's some kind of like critical  issue that something crops up that like  other other people can't fix in time  agent-based instrumentation on the other  hand which some vendors use is an  executable that you have running in the  background on kubernetes or onto the MS  that hosts your app the one of those  sort of perceived benefits of  agent-based instrumentation is it's  entirely automatic you download and  install this thing you run it and it  just captures traces that captures  metrics they keep itself updated the  challenge is it's hard to version is  agents are typically tied to one of your  VM images that you build or something  like that another challenge is that if  for agents that run on say the JVM for  example they do things like monkey  patching and a lot of sort of weird  instrumentation tricks to get data out  of your app this generally works  relatively well but as a developer you  don't always have confidence that's  going to do everything that you want or  you can never be too sure of its  interactions I'm not trying to bash  database instrumentation the automatic  benefits are tangible like they're real  but we've pretty deliberately chosen to  do things in process with a library that  you link to your code we think it's  better for developers because they can  better manage it and they can see how  things work finally it makes the  instrumentation process  little easier for example for GRP see we  depend on some hooks that the GRCC  client libraries exposed to open census  and what you know we conversion those  and be pretty confident they're not  going to change if you're doing agent  based instrumentation and trying to do  things like monkey pat